=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 has a misconfigured iptables FORWARD chain that silently discards all inter-site transit traffic arriving on its eth2 interface (10.50.1.2/24, the r6_r3 WAN link connecting to router3). The chain contains three broad DROP rules targeting eth2 as the input interface: one for TCP, one for UDP, and one for ICMP. The only exception is a very narrow ACCEPT rule that passes TCP port 80 traffic from 172.16.10.0/24 to a single host (172.18.51.1). The drop counters grew visibly during investigation (ICMP: 8→10 packets, UDP: 25 packets) confirming active suppression of every ping and traceroute probe.

The mechanism: all traffic destined for the mswitch2-attached hosts (laptop4, laptop5 on VLAN50 / 172.18.50.0/24; pc15–pc18 on VLAN51 / 172.18.51.0/24) that comes from the rest of the network arrives on router6.eth2 and is immediately dropped in the FORWARD chain before it can be delivered. Similarly, reply packets to traffic originated by mswitch2 hosts return via eth2 and are also dropped, making all outbound connections appear to time out. Router6 can itself reach mswitch2 hosts (those packets use the OUTPUT chain), which is why local gateway pings within the site succeed — but no cross-site traffic flows.

Root cause: iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6.eth2 block all ICMP, TCP, and UDP transit packets from the WAN-side link.

Corrective fix: Remove the three offending rules from router6: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p icmp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`. If a stateful firewall policy is desired, replace them with `-m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` plus a default drop — but do not make the mswitch2 site a one-way black hole.
=== END ===